hadiths[1]{hadithnumber,text}:
"2956","**\nNarrated to us Abdullah ibn Sa‘id al-Ashaj, narrated to us Abu Khalid—meaning Sulayman ibn Hassan—on the authority of Muhammad ibn Ishaq, on the authority of ‘Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Qasim, on the authority of ‘A’ishah (RA), who said:\n\n“The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) departed [from ‘Arafah] at the end of his day when he had prayed Zuhr, then he returned and stayed in Mina during the nights of the days of tashreeq, pelting the jamrah when the sun had passed its zenith, each jamrah with seven pebbles, saying the takbir with each pebble. He would stand by the first and the second jamrah, prolonging his standing and supplicating humbly, then he would pelt the third without stopping at it.”\n\nAbu Bakr (ibn Abi Shaybah) said: This wording—“when he had prayed Zuhr”—appears at first glance to contradict the report of Ibn ‘Umar which we mentioned earlier, namely that the Prophet (ﷺ) departed on the Day of Sacrifice, then returned and prayed Zuhr at Mina. I believe, however, that the meaning of this wording does not actually contradict Ibn ‘Umar’s report. Perhaps ‘A’ishah (RA) intended to say that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) departed at the end of his day when he had prayed Zuhr after having returned to Mina. If the report of ‘A’ishah (RA) is understood in this sense, it does not conflict with the report of Ibn ‘Umar. Moreover, the report of Ibn ‘Umar has a stronger chain of transmission than this report. The report of ‘A’ishah (RA) is of the type we call “mixed” in which words may be advanced or delayed, as in the verse:\n\n“All praise is due to Allah Who sent down upon His servant the Book and made it not deviate” (Qur’an 18:1),\n\nand there are many such examples in the Qur’an; I have explained some of them in my book Ma‘ani al-Qur’an, and I will explain the rest, Allah willing. It is like His saying:\n\n“Certainly We created you, then fashioned you, then said to the angels: ‘Prostrate to Adam’” (Qur’an 7:11).\n\nThus, according to this interpretation, the meaning of ‘A’ishah’s (RA) words is: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) departed at the end of his day, then he returned when he had prayed Zuhr, having advanced the phrase “when he had prayed Zuhr” before “then he returned,” just as Allah the Exalted and Majestic advanced “We created you” before “then fashioned you,” the intended meaning being: “We fashioned you, then We created you.”\n\n---"
